<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>SBR.ConsoleGame</RootNamespace>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\engine\SBR.Engine.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ONE COMPOSER, TWO SURFACES (spec-console-surfaces-2026-08-19 §6.6, K8).

    MarketSheet and MarketDestinations are linked BY SOURCE rather than copied, so the console and
    the laptop cannot print two different names for the same market — which is MarketLabel's own
    stated purpose, unmet until now. They live under unity/ only because the laptop needed them
    first; both are deliberately pure C# over SBR.Engine with no UnityEngine type in either, and
    MarketSheet.cs says so in its own doc comment.

    Linking rather than moving them into the engine is deliberate: moving would change the engine
    and force a rebuild of the tracked SBR.Engine.dll, which this lane must never commit.

    What the console gets for free is the whole spine the spec asks for - S95's six destinations in
    order (§4), the folio (§5), and the matchup-global line numbers that ARE the pick addresses
    (§5.1) - already gated by the laptop's own EditMode suite.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\unity\SBR\Assets\SBR\Runtime\MarketDestinations.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\unity\SBR\Assets\SBR\Runtime\MarketSheet.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
